<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Turnhout (1789)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brabant-revolution">Brabant Revolution</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Turnhout was fought on 27 October 1789 in the town of Turnhout, in the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, where the patriot army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brabant-revolution">Brabant Revolution</a> under Jan Andries van der Mersch defeated Austrian troops. The victory encouraged risings across the provinces, and by December the Austrians had been driven from most of the country, leading to the proclamation of the United Belgian States in January 1790.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-turnhout-1789.jpg" alt="Battle of Turnhout (1789)" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: I. F. Tomkins · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomkins,_Peltro_William,_The_young_hero_of_Turnhout_who_so_manfully_distinguished_himself_on_the_27th_Octr._1789_Le_Jeune_Heros_de_Turnhout,_Victoire_des_Brabacons_sur_les_Autrichiennes_le_27_8bre_1789_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Joseph II, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, imposed on the Austrian Netherlands a wide programme of reforms, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephinism">Josephinism</a>, intervening in the internal affairs of the Church and reforming law and administration to strengthen the state. Many saw these edicts as an attack on their rights, and protest spread. In January 1789 the states of Brabant and Hainaut refused to pay imperial taxes; the government occupied the County of Hainaut and on 18 June 1789 suspended the rights guaranteed to the Duchy of Brabant.</p><p>The military governor, Count Richard d'Alton, concentrated his troops in De Kempen and around Liège, leaving much of the country thinly defended. In May 1789 the secret society Pro aris et focis was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, while an army of Brabançons gathered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. It moved into Hoogstraten, where Henri Van der Noot read the Manifesto of the People of Brabant, declaring that the emperor had not respected the rights of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joyous-entry-of-1356">Joyous Entry of 1356</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In October one of the two divisions of the patriot army marched towards Brabant and easily took Turnhout on the 25th. Learning that an Austrian force was approaching, van der Mersch judged that a battle in open country would be futile and chose to hold the town and fight in the streets. With the help of Emmanuel-Joseph Van Gansen, son of a brewer in Westerlo, the townspeople built barricades, and defenders took position in the square and near a mill at the town's entrance. The Austrians, poorly prepared for street fighting, retreated after five hours, leaving five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> behind.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>It was the first engagement of the revolution, and news of it spread resistance across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and Brabant. Ghent, Diest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tienen">Tienen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> fell to the rebels, Austrian forces withdrew to the Duchy of Luxembourg, and Brabant declared its independence on 31 December. The battle is commemorated each year with a spectacle in the Gasthuisstraat, where the fighting was fiercest, and a memorial by P. Brozius, dating from the centenary of 1889, stands in the market of Turnhout.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Turnhout (1789)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1789 exiles organised an army at Breda, defeated the Austrians at <b>Turnhout</b> in October, and by December drove them from most of the country.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-belgian-states">United Belgian States</a></li></ul></section>
